# Release note

A release note tells the people who use Odoo what changed for them, in their
words, and takes them straight to it. It is written in the pipeline because
that is where the context is: the ticket, the grill, the diff, the test cases.
Written later, at deploy time, it would be vaguer.

## How it reaches users

The engine is the `release_notes` module of
[okolovmark/nixodoo-addons](https://github.com/okolovmark/nixodoo-addons),
pulled through `addons.yaml`: the Release Note model, its views, the Publish
button and the loader. It ships no notes. The project's notes are files in its
holder module, `{{ module_prefix }}_release_notes` (next section).

1. This skill writes `{{ module_prefix }}_release_notes/notes/<key>.html` in the addons
   worktree `<WT>`. It ships in the same PR as the code and is reviewed with it.
2. The deploy updates `{{ module_prefix }}_release_notes` (the PR changed a file in it),
   which runs the engine's loader. It creates the **Release Note** record
   **unpublished**. It never overwrites a note that already exists, so later
   edits are made in Odoo. A file it cannot read (no `---` header, no `key` or
   `title`, not UTF-8, a key an earlier file already has) gets no record: the
   loader logs `Release note file <name> skipped: <why>` as an ERROR and the
   update goes on, so a bad note never stops a deploy and is never announced
   either.
{% if prod_ssh_host %}
3. The `deploy` skill (Step 7.5) stamps **Deployed by** with the person who
   deployed.
{% else %}
3. Whoever deployed sets **Deployed by** to themselves on the note: a Release
   Notes manager can, while it is empty.
{% endif %}
4. That person opens the note in Odoo (Release Notes menu), reads it, checks the
   buttons, and presses **Publish**. Only they can. Publishing sends an OdooBot
   direct chat with a link to every active internal user who can read at least
   one of the note's models. No email.

## The holder module

`{{ module_prefix }}_release_notes`, in the project's addons repo, holds the notes and
no logic:

- `__manifest__.py`: depends on `release_notes`, loads
  `data/release_note_load.xml`, carries an `author` and a `license`{% if odoo_version == '20.0' %} (Odoo 20
  warns about a manifest without either){% endif %};
- `data/release_note_load.xml`, outside any `noupdate` block so that it runs on
  every update:
  `<function model="release.note" name="_load_release_notes"><value>{{ module_prefix }}_release_notes</value></function>`;
- `notes/`, one `<key>.html` per task;
- a test that parses every shipped file: the loader only skips a bad file, the
  test fails on it.

An addons repo that has none yet gets it once, before the first note; the
recipe is in the engine's README (`src/nixodoo-addons/release_notes/README.rst`,
Configuration). That is a code change like any other: through dev and the QC
gate, never in Phase 4b, where only a note file leaves the QC pass standing.
Installing the holder brings the engine with it.

## When there is no note

Write none when nothing changes for a person using Odoo: compute fixes with no
visible effect, refactors, migrations, tooling, tests, docs. Say so in the
phase report ("Release note: none — no user-visible change"). A bug fix users
noticed does get a short note ("Fixed: …").

## The file

Name: the task key in lower case with `_`: `{{ ticket_prefix | lower }}_1234.html`. One note per task.
If a later PR of the same task changes what users see and the note is not yet
on prod, edit the same file; once it is on prod, the text is fixed in Odoo.

```html
---
key: {{ ticket_prefix }}-1234
title: Purchase orders show the vendor's lead time
summary: The PO form now shows how many days the vendor needs, and warns when the date is too early.
tags: Purchase Orders
models: purchase.order
---
<h2>Vendor lead time on the purchase order</h2>
<p>When you pick a vendor, the order now shows how many days they need to
deliver. If the expected date is earlier than that, a yellow warning tells you
before you confirm.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Where:</strong> Purchase → Orders → any draft order.</li>
  <li><strong>What to do:</strong> nothing; the warning is a hint, not a block.</li>
</ul>
<p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="{{ action_href }}purchase.purchase_rfq">Open Requests for Quotation</a></p>
```

Header (all lines `name: value`, between the two `---` lines):

| Field | Rule |
|---|---|
| `key` | The task key, exactly. The loader matches on it. |
| `title` | What changed, from the user's side, ≤ 70 chars. Not the ticket title. |
| `summary` | One sentence, the line users see in the chat. |
| `tags` | Comma-separated work areas. The engine seeds Sales, Purchase Orders, Inventory, Manufacturing, Projects, Accounting, HR, Maintenance, Quality, Shipping, CRM: use these names; any other name creates a new tag, so add one only for a real new area. |
| `models` | Comma-separated technical model names, 1–3: the business documents the change is about. They decide who is notified (users who can read one of them), so not every model the diff touches: never `res.partner`, `product.product`, `mail.thread` unless the change is about them. |

## The body

Write for the person in the warehouse, the buyer, the accountant, not for a
developer. English, whatever the conversation's language: it is prod content.

- **Lead with the benefit**, one `<h2>` per change (most notes have one).
- **Say where it is** (menu path) and **what to do** in one or two short
  sentences each. Plain words: "the order", "the button", never field or model
  names.
- **A button to the change** when there is a screen to open:
  `<a class="btn btn-primary" href="{{ action_href }}<module>.<action_xml_id>">…</a>`.
  The link carries the action's XML id and resolves when clicked; nothing
  checks it at load. Find the id with `find-code` or the module's views. Write
  every button as `btn btn-primary`; any other `btn` class is turned into it
  when the note is saved, because the backend draws `btn-secondary` and the
  outline variants as bare links. No buttons to records that exist only on one
  database.
- **Before/after** in a sentence when a behaviour changed ("Before, … Now, …").
- Short. A note people read in 30 seconds beats a complete one nobody reads.
- No scripts, styles, images or iframes: the body goes through Odoo's HTML
  sanitizer, which keeps headings, paragraphs, lists, links and the `btn`
  classes.

## Check before handing it on

The note is written after the QC gate, so no review and no test run reads it:
these checks are the only ones it gets.

- The header parses: `key`, `title` present; tags and models as above.
- Every `models:` name is a model on prod: the loader drops an unknown name
  without a word, and a note left with no model cannot be published. The
  loaded note (below) shows in **Models** what stayed.
- Every button's XML id exists in the PR or on prod
  (`python3 .claude/skills/find-code/lsp.py who <xml_id>` or a grep of the
  module's XML).
- Load it on the session DB (`<slug>` and `<URL>` are the worktree env's,
  `worktree-env` skill):
  ```bash
  systemctl --user stop odoo{{ service_suffix }}-wt@<slug>.service
  bash "${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/worktree-env/scripts/wt-test.sh" <slug> -u {{ module_prefix }}_release_notes
  systemctl --user start odoo{{ service_suffix }}-wt@<slug>.service
  ```
  (`-i` on a DB whose dump is older than the holder: `wt-test.sh` refuses the
  `-u` and names it.) The output carries no `Release note file … skipped`
  line, and the note is in Release Notes at `<URL>`: read it there and open
  each button. A manager gets the body in the text editor, where a click on a
  button shows its link and that link opens the screen; a user without the
  manager group clicks the button itself.
- The file is part of the PR's diff (`git -C <WT> status`).
